WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:01.233 --> 00:00:02.400
Back in the
1960s, Jane Goodall,

00:00:02.400 --> 00:00:06.934
with no formal training
in science at the time.

00:00:06.934 --> 00:00:10.266
I mean, holding aside her
four-year-old exploits.

00:00:10.266 --> 00:00:12.734
The fact is, in the
real world, people

00:00:12.734 --> 00:00:14.000
look, well, what's your resume?

00:00:14.000 --> 00:00:16.100
Where did you get your
degrees in science?

00:00:16.100 --> 00:00:17.600
She had no formal
training in science

00:00:17.600 --> 00:00:19.967
and she went alone into
the Tanzanian jungle

00:00:19.967 --> 00:00:22.100
to study chimpanzees.

00:00:22.100 --> 00:00:25.300
Which, by the way, had
never been done before.

00:00:25.300 --> 00:00:31.633
So I asked her, how and why
she found herself on that path,

00:00:31.633 --> 00:00:33.233
without having any
science background at all?

00:00:33.233 --> 00:00:34.233
Certainly not anthropology.

00:00:34.233 --> 00:00:35.967
Let's check it out.

00:00:35.967 --> 00:00:39.266
So in the 1960s, there's of
course, we're in the Cold War,

00:00:39.266 --> 00:00:43.166
we're going to the moon, and
you're thinking about chimps.

00:00:43.166 --> 00:00:46.500
I'm desperately trying
to get into their world

00:00:46.500 --> 00:00:48.200
and find out about them.

00:00:48.200 --> 00:00:51.200
If no one had really
done that before,

00:00:51.200 --> 00:00:53.433
then you're not following
in anyone's footsteps.

00:00:53.433 --> 00:00:56.800
No, and my mentor, Dr.
Lewis Leakey, you know,

00:00:56.800 --> 00:00:59.567
paleontologist, spent
his life searching

00:00:59.567 --> 00:01:03.667
for the remains of the
earliest humans in Africa.

00:01:03.667 --> 00:01:05.300
So not even he is
looking for chimps.

00:01:05.300 --> 00:01:08.400
Or he's looking for
something en route to humans.

00:01:08.400 --> 00:01:12.500
His argument was, OK,
about 60 million years ago,

00:01:12.500 --> 00:01:15.367
there's an ape-like,
human-like creature,

00:01:15.367 --> 00:01:22.767
and if you uncover a
fossil of an early human,

00:01:22.767 --> 00:01:25.533
you can tell an awful lot
from the muscle attachment,

00:01:25.533 --> 00:01:28.734
from the wear on the
teeth, from the tools

00:01:28.734 --> 00:01:30.100
associated with their living.

00:01:30.100 --> 00:01:33.834
So you can learn a lot about
the behavior, social behavior.

00:01:33.834 --> 00:01:35.533
That doesn't fossilize.

00:01:35.533 --> 00:01:40.133
So his theory was if Jane
sees behavior that's similar

00:01:40.133 --> 00:01:44.266
or the same between
chimpanzees today and humans

00:01:44.266 --> 00:01:49.767
today, perhaps that same
behavior was brought by humans

00:01:49.767 --> 00:01:53.200
and by chimps along a
long evolutionary journey,

00:01:53.200 --> 00:01:57.734
and originated in that
ape-like, human-like creature.

00:01:57.734 --> 00:02:00.567
that's why he sent
me out to Gombe.

00:02:00.567 --> 00:02:03.300
But he didn't know anything
about the field work.

00:02:03.300 --> 00:02:05.767
He just sent me
off on my own to go

00:02:05.767 --> 00:02:07.633
and find out about the chimps.

